19:
Art (sippa) is of two kinds: the art of householders and the art of those who have gone forth (monastics). Among these, the art of householders refers to crafts such as gem-cutting and goldsmithing, which are free from harming others and free from unwholesome deeds. This art is called a blessing (maṅgala) because it brings about welfare in this world. The art of those who have gone forth refers to the preparation and repair of a recluse's requisites, such as the arranging and sewing of robes. This is praised in various places in such a manner as: "Here, monks, a monk is skilled and diligent in the various duties to be done for his fellow practitioners." This art, which is called "a quality that makes one a protector," should be understood as a "blessing" because it brings the benefit and happiness of both worlds to oneself and others.

Discipline (vinaya) is of two kinds: the discipline of householders and the discipline of those who have gone forth. Among these, the discipline of householders is the abstinence from the ten unwholesome courses of action. This discipline, when well-trained by remaining established in the virtue of good conduct without falling into defilement, is called a blessing because it brings about the benefit and happiness of both worlds. The discipline of those who have gone forth is the non-committing of the seven classes of offenses. This discipline is also to be well-trained in the same manner as mentioned before. Alternatively, the fourfold purification of virtue (catupārisuddhisīla) is the discipline of those who have gone forth. Because it is the cause for attaining mundane and supramundane happiness by training well so as to reach Arahantship while established in that discipline, it should be understood as a "blessing."

Well-spoken speech (subhāsitā vācā) is speech that is free from faults such as lying. The Blessed One said: "Monks, speech endowed with four factors is well-spoken." Or, only speech free from frivolous talk is well-spoken. The Blessed One said:
"The virtuous say that well-spoken speech is the best;
One should speak the Dhamma, not non-Dhamma—that is the second;
One should speak what is pleasant, not unpleasant—that is the third;
One should speak the truth, not falsehood—that is the fourth."

This speech should also be understood as a "blessing" because it brings about the benefit and happiness of both worlds. Since this speech is included within discipline (vinaya), one should take only the discipline without taking this separately when discipline is mentioned. Alternatively, without such effort, the speech of teaching the Dhamma to others should be understood here as "well-spoken speech."

24:
“The gift of Dhamma excels all gifts;
The taste of Dhamma excels all tastes;
The delight in Dhamma excels all delights;
The destruction of craving conquers all suffering.” (Dhammapada 354).

Among those two kinds of gifts, the status of material gift as a blessing has already been stated. The gift of Dhamma, however, is called a ‘blessing’ because it is the proximate cause for qualities such as penetrating the meaning. For this was said by the Blessed One: “Monks, in whatever way a monk teaches the Dhamma to others in detail as he has heard it and as he has learned it, in that very way he becomes one who experiences the meaning and experiences the Dhamma in that Dhamma” (Dīgha Nikāya 3.355, Aṅguttara Nikāya 5.26).

Dhammacariyā (living according to Dhamma) means practicing the ten wholesome courses of action. As it is said: “Householders, there are three ways in which there is living according to Dhamma, living in harmony, by way of the body,” and so on. That living according to Dhamma should be known as a ‘blessing’ because it is the cause for rebirth in the heavenly world. For this was said by the Blessed One: “Householders, by reason of living according to Dhamma and living in harmony, some beings here, with the breakup of the body, after death, are reborn in a good destination, in a heavenly world” (Majjhima Nikāya 1.441).

Relatives (ñātakā) are those related to the pairs of grandparents up to the seventh generation, whether on the mother’s side or the father’s side. Supporting those relatives who have come to one, having been afflicted by the loss of wealth or the loss of health, with food, clothing, money, grain, etc., according to one’s strength, is called a ‘blessing’ because it is the cause for present benefits such as praise and for attaining special future benefits such as going to a good destination.

Blameless actions (anavajjāni kammāni) refer to wholesome actions of body, speech, and mind, such as undertaking the Uposatha precepts, performing service (veyyāvacca), planting groves and forests, and building bridges. These are called ‘blessings’ because they are the cause for attaining various kinds of welfare and happiness. In this regard, suttas such as “Visākhā, it is possible that some woman or man here, having observed the Uposatha endowed with eight factors, with the breakup of the body, after death, may be reborn in the company of the Cātumahārājikā devas” (Aṅguttara Nikāya 8.43) should be remembered.
